# Migration files are checksummed byte-for-byte by migrations/runner.py. With
# Git's Windows default core.autocrlf=true and no .gitattributes, the same
# committed file is CRLF in a Windows checkout and LF on Render — two sha256
# values for one migration. Checksum drift makes the runner refuse to apply
# ANYTHING, so a line ending was enough to block every deploy. Pinning eol=lf
# stops it at source; runner.equivalent_checksums() is the belt to this braces,
# for databases whose ledger already recorded the other form.
*.sql text eol=lf

# Same reasoning: the trust store is read and compared as text, and a stray CR
# inside a base64 public key is not something anyone should have to debug.
trusted_ap2_clients.json text eol=lf
